---
/**
 * Legacy URL: /concrete-countertops/ (WP page ID 80).
 *
 * The prose is the owner's, from the recovered page, lightly reset for the new
 * layout — his argument, his claims, his voice.
 *
 * The old page said "since 1993" here. Owner-corrected to 1991 on 2026-09-15:
 * one founding date, everywhere, matching BUSINESS.founded and the CSLB record.
 * Prefer the literal "since 1991" in body copy over YEARS_IN_BUSINESS — it
 * never goes stale.
 */
import ServiceLayout from '../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import Img from '../components/Img.astro';
import { A } from '../lib/assets';
import { withBase } from '../lib/urls';

/*
  The lit tops get a band of their own rather than a slot in the gallery grid.
  Two reasons. The photographs are 95% black, so dropped into the white
  showcase grid they read as underexposed mistakes; on black they read as the
  product. And the owner rates this as the thing that sells the trade, which a
  thumbnail in row three does not say.
*/
const lit = [
  {
    asset: A.fiberOpticConcreteCountertopLitAtNight,
    caption: 'Lights down: the fiber ends carry the whole room.',
  },
  {
    asset: A.colorChangingFiberOpticConcreteCountertop,
    caption: 'Three zones, three colors, one slab.',
  },
];

const showcase = [
  { asset: A.blackConcreteCountertops,
    caption: 'Black concrete countertops, cast and polished.' },
  { asset: A.blackConcreteCountertopsWithBarbecue,
    caption: 'The same run carried through an outdoor barbecue counter.' },
  { asset: A.kitchenConcreteCountertopsReadyForSinkInstall,
    caption: 'Kitchen countertops cast in place and ready for the sink to drop in.' },
  { asset: A.backyardCustomConcreteCountertopBarbecueSetup,
    caption: 'Backyard barbecue counter with a cast concrete top.' },
  { asset: A.concreteCountertopCloseup,
    caption: 'Close on the finish: concrete takes a polish that reads as stone.' },
];

const gallery = [
  A.closeupOutdoorConcreteCountertop,
  A.concreteKitchenCounterCloseup,
  A.backyardConcreteCountertopFireFeature,
  A.backyardConcreteCountertopFireFeature2,
  A.outdoorConcreteCountertopProgressShot,
  A.outdoorConcreteCountertopProgressShot2,
];
---

<ServiceLayout
  title="Concrete Countertops"
  eyebrow="Custom Concrete"
  intro="Most people are only now hearing about concrete as an option for countertops. CPF has been building them from scratch since long before they became the new trend in modern luxury."
  description="Custom concrete countertops in Thousand Oaks, Newbury Park and Ventura County: cast kitchen counters, polished finishes, fiber-optic lit tops, outdoor barbecue and fire-feature counters. Building them from scratch since 1991. CA License #878989, C-29 / C-8."
  headerAsset={A.blackConcreteCountertops}
  serviceName="Custom concrete countertop design and casting"
  showcase={showcase}
  gallery={gallery}
  galleryHeading="Kitchen, barbecue and fire-feature tops"
>
  <section slot="feature" class="bg-[#08080b] py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6">
      <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-cpf-yellow">
        Fiber optics in the concrete
      </p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-[var(--text-h2)] font-semibold uppercase text-white">
        Fiber-optic tops: a counter that turns into a night sky
      </h2>
      <hr class="rule-red mt-4" />

      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/80">
          Hundreds of fiber-optic strands are cast into the slab itself, not
          fixed underneath it afterwards, and polished back flush with the
          surface. In daylight it is a concrete counter and the fibers are
          invisible. Turn the kitchen lights off and the top is the only thing
          in the room.
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/80">
          The fibers run in zones off their own illuminators, so one top can
          show more than one color at a time. Nothing about it is off-the-shelf
          — the layout, the density and the colors are all decided before a
          single pour, which is the whole argument for a cast top over a
          quarried slab.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="mt-12 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-8">
        {lit.map((item, i) => (
          <li>
            {/* Blue cast under the frame so the photograph reads as emitting
                light rather than sitting in a hole cut out of the band. */}
            <div class="shadow-[0_0_90px_-25px_rgba(60,105,255,0.75)]">
              <Img
                asset={item.asset}
                displayW={620}
                sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 600px"
                label={`lit countertop ${i}`}
                lightbox
                class="!bg-black ring-1 ring-white/10"
                imgClass="aspect-[3/2] w-full object-cover"
              />
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/60">{item.caption}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>The sky is the limit</h2>
  <p>
    Rest assured that we at CPF have been building concrete countertops from
    scratch since long before they became the new trend in modern luxury. The
    sky is the limit, so let us help you design something that stands above the
    rest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The skill of the contractor is everything in this trade.</strong>
    Below are examples of projects that have made some very happy customers.
  </p>

  <h2>Granite versus custom concrete</h2>
  <p>
    If you have spent any time researching remodeling options lately, then
    surely you have seen all the rage about concrete countertops — more
    specifically, the debate over who wins: granite or custom concrete. There
    are a lot of factors at play, and in the end it all comes down to
    preference. But one thing is unarguably true: concrete countertops are the
    most durable and versatile choice that your money can buy.
  </p>
  <p>
    CPF has been pushing the envelope of what can be done with custom concrete
    since 1991, and has the track record you are looking for in a contractor you
    can trust with your custom concrete countertop job.
  </p>

  <h3>Why versatility is the real argument</h3>
  <p>
    A slab of granite is quarried and then you work around what it is. A
    concrete top is cast, which means the shape, the edge profile, the color and
    the thickness are all decisions rather than constraints — including shapes a
    slab cannot do at all. The serpentine outdoor bar on the
    <a href={withBase('/featured-project-dos-vientos-villa/')}>Dos Vientos Villa</a>
    project is the clearest example: a continuous curved counter with an
    integrated sink, cooler and barbecue, which is simply not a thing you cut
    out of stone.
  </p>

  <h2>Owner supervised, fully insured</h2>
  <p>
    Countertop work happens inside a kitchen that somebody is trying to live in.
    <strong>Each project is owner supervised</strong>, and CPF is
    <strong>licensed, insured and bonded</strong> under C-29 masonry and C-8
    concrete.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>
